ancement so that would be the idea of  you know bootstrapping our metadata up  into something higher quality higher  accuracy audience driven priorities  things like enhanced search like we've  seen examples of today as well we also  can organize these ideas into sort of  more tech centric themes and again it's  a tangled mess of knots where our  traditional disciplines no longer make  as much sense  and it also means a tremendous  opportunity for us to be more efficient  about how we develop so I'm going to  zoom in here to specifically the  American Women's History Initiative  concepts that were developed and what  we're looking at here on the left hand  side is the group of about four people  who are working on the American Women's  History cohort and if you track the  purple lines on top over the right hand  side those were the ideas that they came  up with don't worry about reading the  fine print too much down here we have  other cohorts that came up with ideas  that were strongly related to or  applicable to American women's history  and so you can see those popping up on  the right hand side and then basically  what you have here is a list of ideas  that do the most good for the most  people based on the interests expressed  at this workshop as opposed to what  might be the project's represented on  top which maybe have all the virtue in  the world in and of itself but does not  offer as much opportunity for us to  build upon we're gonna track backwards  now and and see how well this method  might be useful at this layer we can see  all the key internal stakeholders so  what unit units across the Smithsonian  are interested in these particular  concepts what was that individual origin  concept that got truncated into these  more overarching themes and essentially  we have all the details that are so  difficult in getting traction on a given  project so this isn't an argument to  think beyond our grasp  but it is an argument to initiate and  design projects with lots of open doors  for expansion and new opportunities when  we come to the conclusion in these  collaborations and I think that's what  this type of mindset might help with and  I think the point here is that we can  overlay any type of content with this  concept so these were a number of  different proposals that stemmed some  from the workshop and some were ideas  that were in progress originally and  we're proposed to as collaborations with  Google but the exciting thing is that we  could take any other idea and basically  do an analysis of how many how effective  it would be for our overarching goals  and we can be pretty flexible in terms  of which content we choose to run with  in this respect and in terms of bias and  I think the the shackles example which  is really important on one hand it's a  huge liability when we mislabel an  object like that and it can be hugely  damaging I don't want to underestimate  the severity of that as an issue but I  also think that there is a flip side and  an opportunity there and in the end what  happened is that surfaced a deficiency  in the model that's being used and I  think that's a really critical role that  we can play and cultural heritage in  general with our data on the periphery  of what general tech companies have  access to we're really going to be the  critical last mile in making a lot of  what they want to do possible so it's  basically I think we should I'm  advocating for us to value what we have  to offer the rest of industry as much as  we're excited about how we can use this  tech in in-house  [Applause]  [Music]

